8499497,2024-03-07,Checking or savings account,Savings account,Managing an account,Deposits and withdrawals,"Marcus Bank reversed a withdrawal of available funds to a verified external bank account. A suspicion of fraud was not involved. 

On Monday XX/XX/XXXX I initiated from Marcus Bank an ACH withdrawal of {$12000.00} to XXXX XXXX ( a division of XXXX XXXX ). 

Marcus Bank advertises that ACH transfers scheduled before XXXX XXXX ET should arrive the same day at its destination barring a delay due to certain conditions. 

The next day, Tuesday XX/XX/XXXX, I saw that Marcus Bank had not transferred the funds. I suspected the reason was because I had recently linked XXXX  ( XXXX ) XXXX to my Marcus Bank account.

I phoned Marcus Bank that evening to learn why the transfer had not been executed, and whether there was an issue with my account. Twice in the past Marus Bank had put holds on my funds without notification. The customer service representative said everything appeared in order, and that the transfer should soon go through. 

The next day, Wednesday XX/XX/XXXX, I saw that once again the funds had not been transferred out of my Marcus Bank account. I phoned its customer service that afternoon and requested to speak with its transfer department. I explained that I suspected that the issue holding up the transfer was because the external account was recently linked and Marcus Bank has a practice of wanting to verify ownership of these newly linked accounts. And again, the customer service representative said there should be no issue with the transfer, and would not let me speak with the transfer department. 

Sure enough, later that day I received a call from Marcus Banks transfer department requesting that I hold a three-way call with XXXX ( XXXX ) XXXX to verify my ownership of that account and to learn how long I had held that account. XXXX XXXX verified my personal information and that the account had been opened since XX/XX/XXXX. 
